iness it  was a software company I had with two  friends and I was making 300 bucks a  month trying to get this thing to go and  I remember when I was graduating I got  opportunities to work with two of the  biggest financial companies in the world  opportunities that my friends were  jealous of like they want these  opportunities opportunities that paid 80  to $100,000 a year starting salary 80 to  100 K starting salary I'm here making  $300 a month can barely survive and  here's an opportunity to just totally  transform my life in a second they're  flying me to New York to recruit me  they're putting me up in the  waldorf-astoria and as I go through the  process I just felt like I had to keep  going with my business you know it  didn't make sense logically if I made my  list reasons to do it reasons not to do  it it didn't make sense logically and my  friends were like what you're crazy  how could you how could you not chase  this why are you doing your business  you're making nothing like you you can't  survive you may be out of a company in  three months and and you know it was  close to me but  some something made me want to keep  going and I'm glad that I did because I  turned that business into an  international company and it had clients  in over 30 countries and I sold the  brand name clients like Johnson Johnson  and NASA and I got my business acquired  and then I hooked up with a venture  capital company and now I'm doing this  and I'm so happy with my life and what  I'm doing right now and I can't imagine  what would have happened if I didn't go  this route and it's funny now because  some of those same friends who I who I  had early on who told me this is the  route we should go and they followed  that path they come back and I see them  now and they're they're telling me I'm  Evan I'm stressed out I'm overworked you  know I hate my life I feel like I'm on  this crazy treadmill and I don't know  what to do and I'm thinking about  starting my own business what do you  think and I'm just like um my mind is  blown and I'm happy to help them out  with their business but that was so  close to being me I almost made that  decision and I would be that guy who  hates his life and his super stressed  out and overworked and and logically  that was the right thing to do but I  listened to my values and what I wanted  to accomplish in my life and I just felt  like even though this doesn't make sense  rationally I have to listen to my heart  and go after this opportunity I got  emailed recently by one of my newsletter  subscribers Phillipe and I really feel  for Philippe Philippe is a seller of  Canon products we sells the camera as a  point click and the higher end cameras  and he's found that his sales have  decreased because people are no longer  buying the point and click cameras  they're just using their phones and he  sees his businesses is struggling now  and I feel for him because it's  it's really hard when you have a  business that's doing well and then  starts to crash and you're not sure what  to do you start to panic it's almost  better to have never experienced that  taste of success because when you get it  you hate losing it and Philippe now is  in the point where he doesn't know what  to do and any rights to me is like do i  how do i get people to be more loyal to  me how do I get people to refer more  customers to me how do I get more  related products in here and he's  wondering should I go with network  marketing should I bring in printers and  sell something totally different should  i implement some kind of customer  loyalty program or Points program here's  the thing Philippe until you understand  what it is that your business stands for  you will never have clarity on any of  these issues you're going to bounce  around from idea to idea grasping at  straws trying to make it happen you have  to first understand why did you start  this business and what are you all about  because until you do that it does